00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.770
Little bit about the
industry we play in.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.060
Moves very slowly.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.440
There are about 270 million
vehicles in operation in the US

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.170
today, so a lot of
vehicles on our roads.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.260
Each of those vehicles has a
lifespan of about 11 years.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.560
So if you do that math,
it takes a real long time

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:20.908
for the industry to change.

00:03:20.908 --> 00:03:22.575
The vehicles that
roll off the lot today

00:03:22.575 --> 00:03:24.780
are going to be out
there for a dozen years.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.900
It's going to take 15,
20 years for the industry

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.010
to change the entire set of
vehicles that are on our roads.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.580
Just to highlight
that some more,

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.390
the first modern vehicle
appeared around 1901.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.010
You can debate what
modern vehicle is,

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.110
but that's about right.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.000
We have about 95 years of that.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.490
'96, the first electric
vehicle happened.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.930
Here in Silicon Valley, we
think a lot about Tesla.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:49.890
We hear a lot about
Elon and his folks.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.650
Man, it's been more than 20
years since electric vehicles

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:53.780
started.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.890
And now, they're a little
over 1% of the industry.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:58.420
So this industry
moves really slow.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.000
While it's moving slowly, we
love the idea of everything

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.580
going autonomous and
electric and maybe flying

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:07.657
or who knows what.

00:04:07.657 --> 00:04:09.490
The reality is, these
vehicles are out there

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.830
with incredible
networks of processors,

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:14.290
running software
that we can upgrade.

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:16.079
So what we've gone
about to do at Derive

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:19.540
and what we offer to
consumers and to fleets

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.060
is the ability to modify
the software inside vehicles

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.430
to make them mission specific.

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.630
So that means, whoever
uses that vehicle,

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:29.380
we'll give you the software
that does that mission best.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:30.400
OK.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.088
It's something we've been doing
for consumers for a long time.

00:04:33.088 --> 00:04:34.630
It's something that
we just recently,

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:37.180
in the last few years, started
doing for enterprise fleets.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:38.860
And we're the only
people that do it.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.600
And we do that
through an IOT device

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:41.977
that goes in the vehicle.

00:04:41.977 --> 00:04:43.810
And that's a real big
differentiator for us,

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.810
because we're the only ones
that write the software.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.393
We're going to talk
about the platform

00:04:47.393 --> 00:04:49.990
the powers that
differentiator for us.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.360
Just to give you a
sense of why this works.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.540
If any of you out there ever use
a Fitbit or a health device--

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.490
I built one of those one time.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:58.480
Yeah.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:04:59.440
Humans.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.740
Really difficult to change
the software of you guys.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:05.387
So we think about this in
terms of enterprise fleets.

00:05:05.387 --> 00:05:06.970
What's happening out
there are devices

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:09.790
that are collecting information
about vehicles, so for example,

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:12.700
how fast the vehicle's
going or who's driving it.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:14.830
Fleet managers are
coaching drivers--

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:18.910
like, hey, I see you're
speeding out there.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.160
Be great if you didn't
do that tomorrow.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.500
It's just like the Fitbit,
like, hey, 9,000 steps

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.660
today, how about 10,000?

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.550
Really hard to change
the habits of humans.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.740
So we're out there trying
to coach these drivers.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:32.030
Drivers are doing the right
thing about 50% to 80%

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:32.530
of the time.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:35.555
And by the way, each
year, about 20% to 30%

00:05:35.555 --> 00:05:37.180
of the drivers in a
fleet leave, so you

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:38.890
get to start again
with that 20% to 30%.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:41.080
So it's really tough
to win this battle

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:44.477
in the automotive industry.

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:46.560
I think the biggest thing
that drives failure here

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.680
is that these behaviors are
at odds with operational KPIs.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.530
So if you think about
drivers in a fleet

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:54.590
that have to make a
number of stops each day--

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:56.100
you got to make
eight stops today.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.350
Sorry, I'm not picking on you.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:59.610
You're just the closest to me.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.250
You're going to make eight stops
today to get your paycheck.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.360
But you're not allowed to speed.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.400
If you get to stop seven, and
it's almost the end of the day,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.770
guess what you're doing
if you get to stop eight?

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:10.000
You're breaking that
speed limit all day.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.070
So one of the challenges is that
operational KPIs are directly

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.820
at odds with how people
drive the vehicle.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:16.975
So because we have write
access to the vehicle,

00:06:16.975 --> 00:06:18.850
we're thinking about
this in a different way.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.957
We modify that the way
the vehicle operates,

00:06:20.957 --> 00:06:22.540
so it doesn't matter
how you drive it.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.790
So for example, I can stop
that vehicle from speeding.

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:28.800
You can put your pedal to the
metal, it's not going to speed.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.860
Here's a case study the talks
about these three things--

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:34.560
saving lives, lowering
environmental impact,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.450
and technology that
pays for itself

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.310
for these big appliances.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:40.920
This is a big major national
fleet that we work with.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.990
Let me just walk you
through this real quick.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:44.970
There are two lines
here-- orange and blue.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.862
Apologies those of you
who are color blind,

00:06:46.862 --> 00:06:48.070
but I'll walk you through it.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:49.890
It will be obvious anyway.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:51.865
The blue line represents
our control group.

00:06:51.865 --> 00:06:54.240
These are vehicles that we
didn't upgrade the software on

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:54.760
at all.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:58.065
And as you can see, over
time, being the bottom axis,

00:06:58.065 --> 00:06:59.190
these people keep speeding.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.687
Speeding violations
just continue.

00:07:00.687 --> 00:07:02.520
And these folks were
coaching their drivers,

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.395
telling them not to
speed, pleading with them

00:07:04.395 --> 00:07:05.640
not to speed.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:07.500
And humans just keep speeding.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.270
The orange line is a test group.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:11.580
This is the set of
vehicles that we modified.

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:14.010
And you can see, at the start,
they weren't doing so well.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:16.890
They started out in a similar
place, they sped a lot.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.640
We upgraded the software
of those vehicles

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:20.015
so they wouldn't
speed at a level

00:07:20.015 --> 00:07:21.713
the fleet deemed unacceptable.

00:07:21.713 --> 00:07:23.130
As you can imagine,
if the vehicle

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.945
can never exceed that speed,
violations go to zero.

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.570
So that's the difference
between trying

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.880
to change the software
of humans and changing

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:33.150
the software of vehicles.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.758
Bit of a video about us.

00:07:34.758 --> 00:07:35.754
