00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:23.140
All right.

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:24.598
That gives you the
lay of the land.

00:09:24.598 --> 00:09:27.880
So for those of you who
are building IOT platforms

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:30.060
or thinking about building
building IOT platforms,

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.050
that's the world
in which we live.

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:34.750
Building IOT devices that
go inside vehicles that have

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.330
a really unique differentiator.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:38.790
That are connected.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:40.517
This is our product
market opportunity.

00:09:40.517 --> 00:09:42.350
Think about your product
market opportunity.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:44.290
It's going to fit a
lot of the same things.

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:46.780
In our case, we deal
with these vehicles that

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:48.520
are running generic software.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:51.100
We live in a commoditized
telematics industry.

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.800
This is just folks who are out
there measuring GPS location

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:55.750
and not able to write
to software of vehicles.

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:58.540
That's about an $18
billion industry.

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:00.790
We brought mission specific
vehicle software upgrades.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.120
That's our big differentiator.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:03.820
But of course, we
also offer telematics

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.350
and all the commodity
features, as well.

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:08.110
We built a platform
that goes after this

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:09.010
and brings it to market.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:11.343
And we're going to walk back
through how we got to that.

00:10:11.343 --> 00:10:13.960
But ultimately, combining
those four things

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:16.300
meant three key features
for our customers one

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:18.220
being fewer collisions.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:20.140
Just drivers out there
having fewer accidents.

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:22.960
And if they're having accidents,
they're much less severe.

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:25.330
Secondly, having a
smaller carbon footprint.

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.490
We reduce the amount of
gas that vehicles use,

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:30.130
which has a huge
sustainability benefit.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:31.300
And lastly, lower costs.

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:33.760
By reducing the amount
of gas that fleets

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:36.653
are consuming in their days,
they're spending a lot less.

00:10:36.653 --> 00:10:38.320
Actually pays for the
technology itself.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:40.090
So a nice little
market dynamic there,

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:42.333
where by making the fleet
less expensive to run,

00:10:42.333 --> 00:10:44.500
they can afford to buy our
technology and then some.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:49.270
When we looked about this
product market opportunity

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:50.728
and selecting a
cloud vendor, there

00:10:50.728 --> 00:10:53.767
were three main
criteria we looked at.

00:10:53.767 --> 00:10:54.850
First was speed to launch.

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:57.790
We saw this huge market
opportunity in this $18 billion

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:00.333
market with this unique
engineering capability,

00:11:00.333 --> 00:11:01.750
and we wanted a
partner that could

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:04.430
support us to get right into the
market as quickly as possible.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.430
We wanted to go capture
this thing while we were

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:08.350
the only ones that could do it.

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:11.020
Second thing, we were getting
huge market demand signals,

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:13.780
because no one else was offering
this to enterprise fleets.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:16.120
So we wanted a partner
that could scale with us.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:17.620
Not just launching
quickly, but then

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:20.450
being able to meet the
demand that came with that.

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:22.390
And lastly, we're
putting connected devices

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:24.160
inside huge machines.

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:27.910
This has got to be secure from
board to cloud and back again.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:30.460
These were the three
main vectors for us.

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:33.160
And obviously, we chose Google.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:34.563
I'm going to kick
it over to Jim.

00:11:34.563 --> 00:11:36.730
Jim is going to talk about
how we went from, really,

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:39.250
nothing to standing
up our first fleets.

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:42.700
Our fleets typically send about
20 million data points a day.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:46.220
And Jim had to stand that up
from scratch, making it happen.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:47.732
So Jim, over to you.

00:11:47.732 --> 00:11:49.440
JIM MARTENS: Hopefully
I'm coming across.

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:50.357
I had to move the mic.

00:11:50.357 --> 00:11:52.910
I learned about a term
called beard noise.

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:56.900
Maybe still getting
a little bit of that.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:12:00.423
It's really impressive all
the tools that Google and GCP

00:12:00.423 --> 00:12:02.590
provides, especially to
those that are trying to get

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:04.330
started with IOT.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:06.940
The communication to IOT--

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:09.190
some of you may know, I know
some arms went up in here

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:11.190
earlier, but it is MQTT.

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:15.160
There are open source libraries
through PAHO and documentation

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:20.200
on getting devices registered
and set up in the cloud

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:20.920
really quickly.

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:23.560
It was important to us to get a
proof of concept and have data

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.207
coming from-- whether even
they be virtual devices--

00:12:26.207 --> 00:12:27.790
and getting those
pushed in the cloud,

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:29.380
and have just that
pathway established,

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:31.570
to understand how we would
take that same technology

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.820
or that same methodology
and bring that over

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:36.250
to the firmware.

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:38.560
For this particular-- I
won't go too deeply into it,

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:44.950
but I'll take one of these
open source Python files

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:49.030
and slightly modify it and
then send some data up.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:52.420
Currently, just to
show end-to-end.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:54.910
We'll first off take--

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:59.320
one thing that's nice about
this particular Python script

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:02.500
is that it establishes
the MQTT connection

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:06.010
and sends that data up into the
cloud that we can then verify.

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:07.810
Like I said, it will
change a few things

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:09.250
when it comes to
the data payload.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.440
This data payload is
very particular to us.

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:12.940
It's something--
the types of things

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:15.890
we care about, especially
when tracking a vehicle.

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:19.600
So we're talking about
latitude, longitude, speed,

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:20.890
those types of things.

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:22.810
We're not going to
get too creative,

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:25.950
but we will update the time.

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:31.403
And maybe we'll change
the latitude, as well.

00:13:31.403 --> 00:13:33.570
Or we can change the course
in this particular time.

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:45.290
All right.

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:48.820
So again, this script is
simply going in and pushing up

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:51.980
the payload that we've selected
for a given virtual device.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:57.505
And then, we would
like to verify

00:13:57.505 --> 00:13:58.630
that that data has gone up.

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:06.820
Here we have our
demo next device 19.

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:10.060
Now, our process for this is
sending data into BigQuery.

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:13.270
It's from BigQuery
that we pull all data

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:16.810
for analysis and
any other processes

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:20.740
that we're going to do analytics
further on down the road.

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:23.380
As you can see, we've recently
sent the most recent message.

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:25.070
The data, we know,
is coming through.

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:27.160
This is not just
helpful for proving

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:29.750
that we have a simple data
pipe going from end to end,

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:32.110
but this proves
particularly useful

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:34.900
when you're doing development
further on down the line.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:36.890
When you're trying
to do testing,

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.280
trying to do any
sort of scaling,

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:41.080
testing, anything
along those lines,

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.120
trying to do it here
before you're actually

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:45.520
trying to push these changes
into something much more

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:48.092
complex, such as firmware
or these IOT devices that

00:14:48.092 --> 00:14:48.925
are inside vehicles.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:56.610
Of course, the GCP
console gives you

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:59.130
access to many, many
things, from being

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:02.400
able to track devices that
are inside your system

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.278
or being able to track
monitoring of said devices.

00:15:05.278 --> 00:15:07.320
Of course, we went from
a development environment

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:11.670
to looking at what is actually
production for a fleet that

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:15.960
is in existence that is sending
out up to 20 million messages

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:17.420
in a given day.

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:24.510
You're able to monitor the
traffic for MQTT traffic,

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:28.570
device operations, errors,
which are luckily quite low,

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:31.260
byte traffic, and what
is the billable data that

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:33.440
is being sent across.

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:36.450
In our particular model,
we're sending already

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:37.518
to the IOT Core.

00:15:37.518 --> 00:15:39.060
And then that data
is being processed

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:42.030
by server-less functions,
or Cloud Functions,

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:44.190
as the case may be.

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:46.950
Again, we tend to see a
similar kind of pattern

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:49.230
in this case, where we get
to see the traffic or Cloud

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:50.772
Functions that are
being instantiated

00:15:50.772 --> 00:15:53.040
over the course of a week.

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:54.840
For ourselves, we
see that there seems

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:56.950
to be a particular
kind of pattern.

00:15:56.950 --> 00:16:01.770
And this is particular
to our IOT use case.

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:05.280
In which case, we have
drivers that are in a fleet,

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:08.400
and their typical traffic
is peak for midday

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:09.900
across the United States.

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:13.560
And then in the evenings, we
see quite a decrease in traffic.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:15.960
For ourselves personally,
even though these devices

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:19.170
aren't sending on a
quick, regular basis,

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:22.870
as if they were actually in
traffic or actually moving,

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:26.670
our devices still send data,
even when they're not active,

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:27.270
necessarily.

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:29.938
Just let us know where they
are and how they're doing.

00:16:29.938 --> 00:16:31.980
And then, if we look at
something on the weekend,

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:35.010
we see less traffic, which is
pretty expectable, one hopes.

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:43.010
Again, there's a wide
range of information

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:46.670
that you can pull from
different cloud platform--

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:47.720
graphs and charts.

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:52.220
Understanding how many different
instances are being served up

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:55.250
to handle the kind of traffic
that's coming through.

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:57.080
One thing that's
really important to us

00:16:57.080 --> 00:17:00.338
is looking for spikes.

00:17:00.338 --> 00:17:02.630
Spikes are what tell us that
possibly something's going

00:17:02.630 --> 00:17:03.130
wrong.

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:05.339
Or there's something that
when we're sending data,

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:07.910
whether it be the BigQuery
or a third party endpoint

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:10.369
for collection and
other processing,

00:17:10.369 --> 00:17:13.190
it's those spikes that let us
know that there's a problem

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:16.240
and alerts us to
some sort of action.

00:17:19.003 --> 00:17:20.420
Another important
tool, especially

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:22.640
if your data s being passed
through Cloud Functions,

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:25.089
is to watch.

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:26.839
For any errors that
might be going across,

00:17:26.839 --> 00:17:30.910
they require further
investigation.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:34.530
A lot of the times, when you're
sending 20 million packages

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:37.060
in a day, you're
going to get errors,

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:39.825
whether that be possibly
internal connection

00:17:39.825 --> 00:17:41.700
errors, where you're
trying to save the data,

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:47.250
or external errors to where
you're trying to send the data.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:50.900
One thing that's really
powerful about IOT is that,

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:54.450
or Pub/Sub, which is
what IOT feeds into,

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:57.450
is that if a message doesn't
reach its end point, if there

00:17:57.450 --> 00:17:59.660
is an error, it isn't lost.

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:01.660
It's sent back to the beginning.

00:18:01.660 --> 00:18:04.650
And generally speaking, this
is what you see with spikes,

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:07.590
is that the data didn't
reach its end point,

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.720
and it's gone back to
the front of the queue

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:12.060
and will try to go
again at a later time.

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:18.060
Metrics and ability
to observe what's

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:20.460
going on within the
system goes much deeper.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:24.960
And typically, this
is within Stackdriver.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:28.770
Stackdriver allows you to
set very specific parameters

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:31.590
on what you may be wanting to
watch, whether that be BigQuery

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:36.920
reads, BigQuery writes, or
anything within the entire JSP

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:38.370
platform that you
need to monitor.

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:44.840
What's more powerful than that
and has proven useful time

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:49.360
and time again is being able to
set alerts within StackDriver.

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:53.420
Such a case might be that
SQL usage is reaching a point

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:55.220
that it might cause problems.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:56.660
And people who've
developed on GCP

00:18:56.660 --> 00:18:59.360
know that there are
set limitations,

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:02.460
resource limitations
and API limitations.

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:05.870
And you can set
alerts and policies

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:11.030
within StackDriver that allow
you to know a problem before it

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:12.500
occurs, before it
stops and causes

00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:15.310
problems within the system.

00:19:15.310 --> 00:19:18.425
And with that, I'll
pass it back to Marco.

00:19:18.425 --> 00:19:19.550
MARCO DELLA TORRE: Awesome.

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:21.840
Shameless round of applause
for Jim for a live demo.

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:22.340
Brave.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.190
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:26.660
Cool.

00:19:26.660 --> 00:19:30.110
So that gives us a sense of
just how we went from zero

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:31.520
to getting the back end going.

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:33.750
And we'll come back to the
architecture a little bit.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:35.083
Scott will walk us through that.

00:19:35.083 --> 00:19:38.150
But once we got the back end
working, got the data flowing

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:41.150
in our system to support
this volume for our fleets,

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:42.950
we started to build
the user experiences.

00:19:42.950 --> 00:19:45.170
So obviously, building
the data flows is great,

00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:47.670
but customers have to have
something to work with.

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:50.560
So here's some examples
of things that we've built

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:52.580
and we're building
for our customers.

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:55.520
Obviously, starting out with
web and mobile data, ways

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:57.620
for drivers to interact
with the system, as well

00:19:57.620 --> 00:20:01.490
as fleet managers, through
dashboards and visualizations.

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:03.200
We offer distracted
driver prevention,

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:04.880
so when the vehicle
comes out of park,

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:06.740
we lock the phone
down, so drivers can't

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:09.395
be sending SMS or making calls.

00:20:09.395 --> 00:20:11.270
It's one of the leading
causes of collisions.

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:12.812
We just take that
right off the table

00:20:12.812 --> 00:20:16.100
by making sure that if they're
in Drive or they're in Reverse,

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:17.220
they're not texting.

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:19.820
Seat belt enforcement,
we're really excited about.

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:22.490
This is one where it prevents
the vehicle from even starting

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:24.290
until the seat belt is on.

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:26.180
We know that most
collisions in a fleet

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:29.447
happen within a really small
radius at the start of a drive.

00:20:29.447 --> 00:20:31.655
That's also the time when
you're least likely to have

00:20:31.655 --> 00:20:33.020
put your seat belt on yet.

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:35.600
And for a driver in a fleet
that's making eight stops a day

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:38.282
or 12 stops a day,
it gets a hassle

00:20:38.282 --> 00:20:40.490
to take that seat belt, put
it on and off every time.

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:41.787
They tend to skip that.

00:20:41.787 --> 00:20:43.370
Well, that's why
they get in accidents

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:44.662
and hurt themselves and others.

00:20:44.662 --> 00:20:46.160
So we're taking
that off the table

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:49.320
by making sure that that
seat belt's on every time.

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:51.305
And lastly, dynamic
speed governing.

00:20:51.305 --> 00:20:53.180
We can offer fixed speed
limits for vehicles,

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:56.210
so that vehicle just never
goes above a certain number.

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:58.570
We're starting to do dynamic
speed governing, as well.

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:01.160
So looking at the location
and heading of the vehicle

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:03.500
and applying the speed
for that road or a speed

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:05.900
relative to that
road to the vehicle.

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:08.290
Nobody needs a delivery vehicle
doing 100 miles per hour

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:10.040
down their street to
get to the next stop.

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:14.120
We stood up the back end.

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:17.240
We started to build
the user experiences.

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:18.420
And it took off.

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:20.750
We work with many of the
nation's largest fleets

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:22.460
now, including one
of the top five.

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:25.190
And that one fleet generates
about 20 million data points

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:27.980
per day and has more
than 25,000 vehicles.

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:30.105
So even as we just started
to build this thing,

00:21:30.105 --> 00:21:30.980
the demand was there.

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:32.930
And it's only taken off since.

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:36.620
So going up to that higher
level of experiences--

00:21:36.620 --> 00:21:38.180
turn it back to
Jim and show us how

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:40.790
we turned that back end of
IOT Core and Pub/Sub data

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:47.710
flows into user experiences
for our customers,

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:50.140
JIM MARTENS: Of course,
just establishing the data,

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:54.170
getting from point A to point
B, isn't entirely that useful.

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:57.130
BigQuery proved to be
one of our best tools

00:21:57.130 --> 00:22:00.100
for identifying devices
in the field that

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:03.100
might be causing problems
or just not behaving

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:04.690
as we expect them to do.

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:07.180
Identifying individual
errors with logging,

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:09.562
while at times was
useful, it didn't

00:22:09.562 --> 00:22:11.020
give us the kind
of insight to help

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:15.430
us dig on particular issues.

00:22:15.430 --> 00:22:17.200
Being able to find
specific vehicles

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:19.330
and how they're behaving
was a simple thing

00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:21.583
to do within BigQuery.

00:22:21.583 --> 00:22:24.250
There are other types of queries
that proved to be quite useful,

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:28.300
like trying to identify
devices that haven't reported

00:22:28.300 --> 00:22:31.000
GPS in some amount of time.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.480
All these devices that
we have, they have LTE,

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:35.640
they have Bluetooth, and
have GPS connections.

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:37.907
So whilst your phone
may also have GPS,

00:22:37.907 --> 00:22:39.490
the device is supposed
to be reporting

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:41.760
its GPS at the same time.

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:44.930
There's a number of reasons that
that might not be happening.

00:22:44.930 --> 00:22:48.580
You may be in the city scape or
a valley that doesn't have GPS.

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:50.530
Or at the end of
the day, the driver

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:52.780
takes the vehicle and
parks it in a garage

00:22:52.780 --> 00:22:54.770
or gets taken in for services.

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:56.270
And so for all
intents and purposes,

00:22:56.270 --> 00:23:00.370
this device disappears
to the fleet, possibly.

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:04.000
So being able to find these
vehicles that aren't reporting

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:05.710
as we expect them
to is something

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:07.840
that's particularly important.

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:10.390
We can then go in and
try and track down what's

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:12.890
happened to those devices.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:16.293
Now, all these tools are
very useful for ourselves,

00:23:16.293 --> 00:23:18.460
internally, while we're in
this development process,

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:21.340
while we're trying to go
through the steps of setting up

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:24.340
this fleet of 25,000 vehicles,
or any fleet that we're

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:25.450
working on.

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:28.570
But it doesn't really add
much value to the user.

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:31.420
As Marco said, that's what we're
really trying to drive towards,

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:34.787
is get something that's useful
for them for tracking data,

00:23:34.787 --> 00:23:37.120
for being able to track their
fleet, manage their fleet,

00:23:37.120 --> 00:23:40.110
gives them real value.

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:43.370
We were able to do this
relatively quickly with, again,

00:23:43.370 --> 00:23:46.390
using just a Google product.

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:49.750
Being able to pump
data from BigQuery

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:53.615
into Data Studio, which allowed
us to, without using BigQuery,

00:23:53.615 --> 00:23:55.990
and it's something that's a
better tool, even internally,

00:23:55.990 --> 00:23:58.090
in some cases, being
able to track down

00:23:58.090 --> 00:24:00.048
specific vehicles, when
they last reported,

00:24:00.048 --> 00:24:01.840
when they last pulled
information that they

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:03.670
were expected to pull.

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:07.580
But then, we wanted
more than that.

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:09.700
And so for that, we
needed to elaborate

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:10.980
on our architecture a bit.

00:24:19.818 --> 00:24:21.291
Back to the slides.

00:24:23.687 --> 00:24:24.770
SCOTT NELSON: Thanks, Jim.

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:31.960
This is Derive's Google
Cloud architecture.

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:33.140
It's simple.

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:35.320
But when we started,
it didn't feel simple.

00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:41.170
For us, we had excitement.

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:42.200
We had confidence.

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:44.980
But we didn't have experience
for building architectures

00:24:44.980 --> 00:24:47.740
that will handle 25
million messages a day.

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:53.890
We took a very iterative
approach to this,

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:55.810
as Marco and Jim has mentioned.

00:24:55.810 --> 00:25:00.130
And I highly recommend
building this in layers

00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:01.950
and building layers upon it.

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:04.930
It's the strategy we did
during development, trying

00:25:04.930 --> 00:25:06.550
to get to market quickly.

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:09.250
And it's the strategy we
leverage for our roadmaps

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:11.890
and for further
capabilities that we have.

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:13.690
And I would like to
walk through the flow

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:16.810
and highlight some of
the big milestones,

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:19.070
starting with our
device in the vehicle.

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:23.170
So as Jim initially
demoed, a big milestone

00:25:23.170 --> 00:25:26.380
is working on your
ingestion and storage

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:29.380
and getting this set up
in a proof of concept

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:31.450
to really start seeing
your data that you

00:25:31.450 --> 00:25:33.370
wish to flow through the cloud.

00:25:33.370 --> 00:25:37.030
And get the tools that you
need to really visualize it.

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:40.910
This was empowering for us
from a development perspective.

00:25:40.910 --> 00:25:44.080
This let us check off a lot of
architectural considerations

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:44.980
that we had.

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:48.920
IOT Core really proved out
the scalability and security.

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:51.280
And Pub/Sub, with
the reliability,

00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:56.390
being able to handle data spikes
that we see from time to time.

00:25:56.390 --> 00:25:58.900
And Cloud Functions, with
the stateless execution

00:25:58.900 --> 00:26:01.480
of horizontal scalability.

00:26:01.480 --> 00:26:06.100
On BigQuery-- BigQuery is a
tool that we leverage a lot.

00:26:06.100 --> 00:26:09.520
The SQL nature,
the ability to see

00:26:09.520 --> 00:26:13.200
data in very cost
effective queries,

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:16.750
is a platform that we really
developed to really see

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:18.770
what we were trying to build.

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:21.290
And it enabled
quite a few things.

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:24.610
It enabled us to
really dive deep

00:26:24.610 --> 00:26:26.080
and try to visualize
what we wanted

00:26:26.080 --> 00:26:29.020
to create in applications.

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:32.170
And identify data
integrity issues.

00:26:32.170 --> 00:26:36.340
Is our devices-- do they
have issues with the metrics

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:38.260
that they're streaming?

00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:41.020
And it helped identify missing
metrics for the capabilities

00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:42.970
that we're trying to capture.

00:26:42.970 --> 00:26:46.120
And this is a milestone that
allowed us to iterate and do

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:49.930
full stack changes in a
pretty efficient manner.

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:53.470
So as we start seeing more
metrics and ingesting them,

00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:56.530
we can work with that
without really impacting

00:26:56.530 --> 00:26:58.520
the rest of the stack.

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:00.910
The next piece that's
pretty critical that

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:02.950
for us, has been a
challenge and is one

00:27:02.950 --> 00:27:07.070
that we're excited to continue
to work on, is data processing.

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:09.200
So for our messages
from vehicles,

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:13.090
these are very specific
events in time.

00:27:13.090 --> 00:27:16.300
I mean, we need to process that
and start having some higher

00:27:16.300 --> 00:27:17.590
abstractions.

00:27:17.590 --> 00:27:19.120
For us, that's trips.

00:27:19.120 --> 00:27:25.030
That's seeing a individual, a
driver, for a window of time,

00:27:25.030 --> 00:27:27.330
go to different locations.

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:28.210
It's trips.

00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:31.690
And it's the insights that
we want to show about trips.

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:33.198
Specifically, that
I'll get to when

00:27:33.198 --> 00:27:34.990
I show off a little
bit of mission control.

00:27:41.265 --> 00:27:42.640
With data processing,
we actually

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:44.590
went through a couple
of iterations of it.

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:47.540
It was definitely
a learning for us

00:27:47.540 --> 00:27:49.770
on how to do this
cost effectively.

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:52.240
And there's quite a few tool
sets that we haven't leveraged

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:55.420
yet that we have in our future.

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:58.052
When it comes to the
actual applications itself,

00:27:58.052 --> 00:28:00.010
this is a little bit more
of in our wheelhouse,

00:28:00.010 --> 00:28:03.040
this is what we've been
developing for years.

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:05.460
So it's really the data
architecture pipeline

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:07.690
that, at least for us
with our experience,

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:11.020
has been a great
learning experience.

00:28:11.020 --> 00:28:13.030
For mission control,
this is our dashboard.

00:28:13.030 --> 00:28:15.760
This is our web application.

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:18.940
And what you're seeing here
is our first insights that we

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:23.590
built. The graph
in the upper left

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:26.560
is showing insights
on engine idle time.

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:27.900
This is a fleet.

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:31.240
And the graph shows the
total amount of time vehicles

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:33.640
were idling each day.

00:28:33.640 --> 00:28:36.070
It's a very simple,
powerful visualization.

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:37.960
And for this
specific use case, we

00:28:37.960 --> 00:28:44.560
have a positive outcome of
the idle time decreasing.

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:46.790
This is caused by a
couple of effects.

00:28:46.790 --> 00:28:49.150
One is leveraging our
ability to upgrade

00:28:49.150 --> 00:28:53.020
the software on vehicles to
help reduce the idle time.

00:28:53.020 --> 00:28:55.810
And then the second is just more
visibility and accountability

00:28:55.810 --> 00:28:57.490
with individual drivers.

00:28:57.490 --> 00:28:59.500
If they understand
their patterns,

00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:03.850
they do have an opportunity
to try to optimize that.

00:29:03.850 --> 00:29:06.700
Similar to our layered approach
of our whole data architecture

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:09.520
pipeline, it comes
up all the way

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:12.160
to the end user
applications, as well.

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:16.030
When we built this, we didn't
have other visualizations.

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:19.270
We didn't have data processing
that went deeper than this.

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:20.680
And as we added
more capabilities

00:29:20.680 --> 00:29:26.530
to mission control, we
continued to add what we need.

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:28.930
This is the sort of
opposite approach.

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:32.630
Instead of insights, this
is going into high fidelity.

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:36.680
And this is looking
specifically at a single chip.

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:41.620
So this is more of a real time
and detailed representation

00:29:41.620 --> 00:29:44.170
of our data processing system.

00:29:46.917 --> 00:29:49.000
In this one, we're still
highlighting a little bit

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:51.370
of the value out of the
system, with showcasing

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:54.052
not just the GPS locations,
but the amount of idle time

00:29:54.052 --> 00:29:54.760
during that trip.

00:29:58.635 --> 00:30:00.260
And from that, I'll
pass it back to Jim

00:30:00.260 --> 00:30:02.260
to really highlight some
of the lessons learned.

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:11.730
JIM MARTENS: And
not on the demo,

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:14.650
but actually just on the slides.

00:30:14.650 --> 00:30:15.150
You got it.

00:30:18.048 --> 00:30:19.590
So I think one of
the biggest lessons

00:30:19.590 --> 00:30:24.270
learned for us really
had to do a lot with how

00:30:24.270 --> 00:30:27.875
best to most cost efficiently
leverage BigQuery.

00:30:27.875 --> 00:30:31.650
BigQuery is an incredible
tool to cheaply store data.

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:33.300
It's a great data warehouse.

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:36.720
And it's simple enough
to pull data from it.

00:30:36.720 --> 00:30:40.590
But we had multiple systems,
both from trip processing

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:45.600
to just simple mapping,
that we were trying to do.

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:50.760
That at times could
be pulling thousands

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:52.650
of queries in a minute.

00:30:52.650 --> 00:30:53.760
We had to change that.

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:55.440
We had to optimize
a lot of the ways

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:58.410
that we both store
data and change data.

00:30:58.410 --> 00:31:00.300
If anybody's worked
with BigQuery,

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:05.248
when you start getting into the
terabytes of data on BigQuery,

00:31:05.248 --> 00:31:07.290
some of the most important
things to keep in mind

00:31:07.290 --> 00:31:09.210
is make sure you use
partition tables,

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:11.460
so that when you're
pulling data in,

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:13.530
you're only pulling
the data you want.

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:15.055
Ultimately, when
it comes to cost,

00:31:15.055 --> 00:31:16.680
that's the biggest
thing with BigQuery.

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:19.440
Typically, you don't need
access to all of the data

00:31:19.440 --> 00:31:21.000
that you're possibly storing.

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:22.860
For us, that can be
particularly true

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:26.190
when we can have hundreds upon
hundreds of columns of data

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:31.510
that we're storing for any
given event that comes across.

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:33.243
So partitioning,
particularly important.

00:31:33.243 --> 00:31:35.160
And making sure that you
leverage your queries

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:36.540
against those partitions.

00:31:36.540 --> 00:31:38.720
Sometimes, we still see
that happen within our own,

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:41.970
where, why did that
one cost that much?

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:44.580
Why did that one singular
query cost that much?

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:45.700
So trying to limit that.

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:48.960
Another really great thing to
do is de-normalization of data.

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:50.460
A lot of us come
from the background

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:52.110
of relational databases.

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:54.330
And in that kind of
environment, you're thinking,

00:31:54.330 --> 00:31:55.930
oh, joins are no big deal.

00:31:55.930 --> 00:31:56.742
We'll do that.

00:31:56.742 --> 00:31:58.200
But again, when
you're running lots

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:02.190
of queries against huge data
sets, you don't want that.

00:32:02.190 --> 00:32:04.810
You want de-normalized data,
to just have it in one table.

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:07.425
So you're not doing joins
on many, many, many queries.

00:32:10.340 --> 00:32:12.890
On top of that, again,
trying to make sure

00:32:12.890 --> 00:32:17.590
that you're only pulling the
data that you absolutely need.

00:32:17.590 --> 00:32:19.317
StackDriver is your friend.

00:32:19.317 --> 00:32:21.650
It's a great way to keep you
from running off the rails.

00:32:21.650 --> 00:32:24.320
That's all too easy.

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:27.240
It's all too easy in GCP to
shoot yourself in the foot.

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:29.630
They give you all the power
you're willing to pay for it.

00:32:29.630 --> 00:32:32.180
And so using StackDriver,
which they give you,

00:32:32.180 --> 00:32:34.900
you can set things up to
keep you on the rails.

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:40.570
And alert you when you've
possibly done something wrong.

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:44.510
Things that we've tried to
avoid, very similar to that,

00:32:44.510 --> 00:32:47.210
leverage StackDriver to try
and avoid hitting or going

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:50.690
over quotas before you find
out because your services

00:32:50.690 --> 00:32:51.620
have started to stop.

00:32:51.620 --> 00:32:56.090
Just shut down because
you've hit your quotas.

00:32:56.090 --> 00:32:58.380
And another thing is,
especially in an environment,

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:01.760
it's important that you
have a good DevOps group,

00:33:01.760 --> 00:33:04.520
or that you have some sort
of a DevOps operation.

00:33:04.520 --> 00:33:07.220
Because again, it's really
easy to set things up

00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:08.895
in your environment on GCP.

00:33:08.895 --> 00:33:10.520
And you can forget
that they're running

00:33:10.520 --> 00:33:12.200
and they're not necessary.

00:33:15.830 --> 00:33:21.910
And again-- a lot of the same.

00:33:21.910 --> 00:33:24.400
Really leverage the tools
within the infrastructure

00:33:24.400 --> 00:33:29.082
to help you keep things in
track with what you expect.

00:33:29.082 --> 00:33:30.540
There's a lot of
different services

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:32.080
and they're always changing.

00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:34.205
And they're always adding
new features to help you.

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:42.520
Google is really quick
and responsive when

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:45.130
you come into issues that
you need to deal with.

00:33:45.130 --> 00:33:47.980
One thing that's been
very helpful to us--

00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:48.940
it goes beyond.

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:50.150
There's the initial
billing that you have,

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:52.025
but you can take all
your billing information

00:33:52.025 --> 00:33:53.530
and you can load
it into BigQuery.

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:55.750
And you can load
that into Data Studio

00:33:55.750 --> 00:33:58.540
and really do deep
drilling to understand

00:33:58.540 --> 00:34:01.670
what the costs and usage is
within your entire organization

00:34:01.670 --> 00:34:03.280
systems are.

00:34:03.280 --> 00:34:04.620
And there's a summary.

00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:06.840
I'll pass it back to Marco.

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:08.800
MARCO DELLA TORRE: Jim,
thank you very much.

00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:10.175
Yeah, we've actually
just started

00:34:10.175 --> 00:34:12.489
piping the data from
billing back into BigQuery.

00:34:12.489 --> 00:34:13.969
It's been a huge asset to us.

00:34:13.969 --> 00:34:15.873
So maybe that's for future demo.

00:34:15.873 --> 00:34:17.290
But we'd really
encourage everyone

00:34:17.290 --> 00:34:20.290
out there doing these
kinds of initial builds

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:22.429
to make that one of
your upfront priorities.

00:34:22.429 --> 00:34:23.469
It's helped a lot.

00:34:23.469 --> 00:34:26.469
Just to run back for a moment.

00:34:26.469 --> 00:34:29.650
I know a lot of you are thinking
about or initially building.

00:34:29.650 --> 00:34:31.900
We'd encourage you to
look at this architecture

00:34:31.900 --> 00:34:35.420
and consider it as a
reference or a resource.

00:34:35.420 --> 00:34:37.780
And if it's
something that you're

00:34:37.780 --> 00:34:40.010
looking at in terms of
building a connected device

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:41.920
and standing up an
initial architecture,

00:34:41.920 --> 00:34:42.670
we'd love to help.

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:44.469
So come talk to us
about our experience.

00:34:44.469 --> 00:34:47.937
I think, as Jim said, we
had some lessons learned,

00:34:47.937 --> 00:34:50.020
but all in all, it was a
pretty smooth experience.

00:34:50.020 --> 00:34:52.385
And especially when
it comes to support,

00:34:52.385 --> 00:34:54.010
I think that's one
of the things for me

00:34:54.010 --> 00:34:56.469
that I would have changed
about our experiences--

00:34:56.469 --> 00:34:59.290
to lean on the Google folks
earlier and more often.

00:34:59.290 --> 00:35:01.810
I think we were probably
a little bit overconfident

00:35:01.810 --> 00:35:03.497
in terms of building things.

00:35:03.497 --> 00:35:06.080
And once we laid on them a bit
more, there was a huge support.

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:08.110
So please take note
of this architecture.

00:35:08.110 --> 00:35:09.265
It's working great for us.

00:35:09.265 --> 00:35:10.640
And we're extending
it from here.

00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:12.640
And if we can help
you with yours,

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:14.410
don't hesitate to reach out.

00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:19.270
Just wanted to end on a summary
of our experience, I guess.

00:35:19.270 --> 00:35:21.190
We needed a cloud partner.

00:35:21.190 --> 00:35:22.690
We had a pretty
amazing opportunity

00:35:22.690 --> 00:35:26.050
and a unique differentiator when
it comes to our feature set.

00:35:26.050 --> 00:35:28.450
So we went out and
shopped around.

00:35:28.450 --> 00:35:30.580
We chose Google for
those three vectors--

00:35:30.580 --> 00:35:31.560
speed to launch.

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:34.210
And it has been less
than a year that we

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:37.660
were from a vision on paper
to a platform supporting

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:40.120
some of the largest
fleets in the country.

00:35:40.120 --> 00:35:41.830
Support for scale,
which is now something

00:35:41.830 --> 00:35:43.540
that we're thankful
for, as we do that.

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:45.040
And then, obviously,
security, which

00:35:45.040 --> 00:35:47.290
we didn't have as much time
to touch on, but something

00:35:47.290 --> 00:35:49.120
we'd love to talk
about, as well.

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:51.370
We have launched successfully
and with some great user

00:35:51.370 --> 00:35:54.880
experiences for drivers and with
dashboards and visualizations,

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:56.740
which we're really
excited about.

00:35:56.740 --> 00:35:59.110
And we're growing the business
from there with Google.

00:35:59.110 --> 00:36:02.020
We learned a ton, most
notably that the reasons

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:04.390
we selected this platform
and this architecture

00:36:04.390 --> 00:36:05.630
actually bore out to be true.

00:36:05.630 --> 00:36:07.660
So it's easy to hear
this stuff upfront.

00:36:07.660 --> 00:36:11.740
It's really relieving when they
actually turn out to be real.

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:13.960
And with huge support from
the Google Cloud team.

00:36:13.960 --> 00:36:16.670
They've really been
pretty amazing about this.

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:17.170
Next up.

00:36:17.170 --> 00:36:19.300
We're really excited
about exploring more

00:36:19.300 --> 00:36:21.010
of the ML side of the platform.

00:36:21.010 --> 00:36:22.810
We're collecting a huge
amount of data now.

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:25.577
And we're doing with it
what we know best, which is,

00:36:25.577 --> 00:36:27.910
working with fleets directly,
showing them their reality

00:36:27.910 --> 00:36:30.860
and then upgrading their
vehicles to get those benefits.

00:36:30.860 --> 00:36:33.130
So they don't have to
rely on driver coaching.

00:36:33.130 --> 00:36:33.990
But we're excited
about what we're

00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:36.365
going to learn from this data
that we don't already know.

00:36:36.365 --> 00:36:38.090
So really excited about that.

00:36:38.090 --> 00:36:39.850
And really appreciate
you letting

00:36:39.850 --> 00:36:42.910
us walk you through
from our first idea

00:36:42.910 --> 00:36:45.488
through the initial
building of the back end,

00:36:45.488 --> 00:36:47.780
